Relevant columns & their possible role  
• `posts_questions.tags`: recognise Python‑2‑only questions (`'<python-2.x>'`,`'<python-2.7>'`, …) and to rule‑out any that also mention Python‑3 (`'<python-3.x>'`, `'<python-3.6>'`, …).  
• `posts_questions.answer_count`: how many answers the question has received – this is the metric we are maximising.  
• `posts_questions.id`, `posts_questions.title`, `posts_questions.body`: handy for displaying / validating the winning post.

Typical filter conditions you will need  
1. Keep questions whose `tags` match a Python‑2 pattern  
   ‑ `REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-2\.')`  
2. Exclude any question whose `tags` contain Python‑3  
   ‑ `NOT REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-3\.')`  

Below are 10 exploratory → final BigQuery queries that gradually narrow‑down the data until the single value you want is returned.  
(Replace back‑ticks exactly as shown; every query is a pure `SELECT`, limited to 20 rows unless it is giving the final aggregate.)

```sql
-- 1. Quick look at how Python‑2 tags appear
SELECT DISTINCT tags
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-2\.')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Sample a few Python‑2 questions and their answer counts
SELECT id, title, tags, answer_count
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-2\.')
  AND NOT REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-3\.')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Same as (2) but ordered to see which ones attracted most answers
SELECT id, title, answer_count
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-2\.')
  AND NOT REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-3\.')
ORDER BY answer_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Verify that none of the top‑20 in (3) contain Python‑3 tag accidentally
SELECT id, tags
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE id IN (
      SELECT id
      FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
      WHERE REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-2\.')
        AND NOT REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-3\.')
      ORDER BY answer_count DESC
      LIMIT 20)
AND REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-3\.');
```

```sql
-- 5. Inspect the body of the currently top‑scoring Python‑2 question
SELECT id, title, answer_count, body
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-2\.')
  AND NOT REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-3\.')
ORDER BY answer_count DESC
LIMIT 1;
```

```sql
-- 6. Distribution of answer counts for Python‑2 questions
SELECT answer_count, COUNT(*) AS num_questions
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-2\.')
  AND NOT REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-3\.')
GROUP BY answer_count
ORDER BY answer_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Cross‑check using LIKE instead of REGEXP
SELECT id, answer_count
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE tags LIKE '%python-2.%'
  AND tags NOT LIKE '%python-3.%'
ORDER BY answer_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Identify all the specific Python‑2.* tags that exist
SELECT DISTINCT REGEXP_EXTRACT(tags, r'<(python-2\.[^>]+)>') AS python2_tag
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-2\.')
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Show the five Python‑2 questions that have ≥30 answers
SELECT id, title, answer_count
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-2\.')
  AND NOT REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-3\.')
  AND answer_count >= 30
ORDER BY answer_count DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. FINAL: the maximum number of answers a pure Python‑2 question ever received
SELECT MAX(answer_count) AS highest_answer_count_python2_only
FROM `bigquery-public-data.stackoverflow.posts_questions`
WHERE REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-2\.')        -- must be Python 2
  AND NOT REGEXP_CONTAINS(tags, r'<python-3\.');   -- must NOT involve Python 3
```

Run query 10 to obtain the single value requested—the highest answer count for a Stack Overflow question that is specifically about Python 2 and contains no reference to Python 3 in its tag set.
